 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A-suffix parts are unidirectional and carry a cathode band. CA-suffix parts are bidirectional, carry no band, and share the row of figures of the matching A part, except that at VR of 10 V or below their IR limit is double. The forward voltage and forward surge ratings (Fig. 6) apply to unidirectional parts only."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulse rating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The 400 W figure is for a single 10/1000 µs pulse: a 10 µs rise to the peak and a decay to half the peak at 1 ms (Fig. 3). Each part's clamping voltage VC is a maximum at its own peak current IPP of that pulse. Shorter pulses allow more power and longer ones less: about 8 kW at 0.1 µs down to about 0.4 kW at 1 ms (Fig. 1)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Derating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Peak pulse power and current stay at 100 % up to Ta = 25 ℃ and fall linearly to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 2). Steady dissipation PM(AV) is 1.0 W up to 75 ℃ and falls linearly to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Forward surge",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A unidirectional part takes 40 A for one 8.3 ms half sine from TJ max; the allowed peak falls with repeated cycles at 60 Hz to about 12 A at 100 cycles (Fig. 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and capacitance",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Typical IR is below 5 µA for parts above 12 V; the table maximum is 1 µA from SMAJ11A up and as much as 800 µA at the lowest voltages. Junction capacitance falls with breakdown voltage and with reverse bias: at zero bias it runs from about 3000 pF (uni) at 5 V to about 50 pF at 450 V (Fig. 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and failure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Glass-passivated junction; molding compound rated UL 94V-0; matte tin, lead-free plating; halogen free and RoHS compliant. Terminals withstand 260 ℃ for 40 s in soldering. Whisker testing follows JEDEC JESD201A tables 4a and 4c. The typical failure under excess voltage or current is a short."
  }
 ],
